\details{
ggcarto gives ggplot2 one consistent way to colour a variable, built around
the things thematic mapping actually needs.
\itemize{
\item \strong{One engine.} \code{\link[=scale_map]{scale_map()}} covers continuous ramps, classed intervals,
categories, ordered categories and ranks. Switching between them is a
change of \code{type}, not a rewrite.
\item \strong{Real class intervals.} Quantile, natural breaks, Fisher, k-means,
standard deviation, head/tails, boxplot, maximum-gap and more, via
\code{\link[=breaks_style]{breaks_style()}} and friends. They are plain functions, so they also drop
into \code{\link[ggplot2:scale_colour_continuous]{ggplot2::scale_fill_binned()}}.
\item \strong{Palettes from everywhere.} \code{\link[=as_palette]{as_palette()}} resolves grDevices,
RColorBrewer, viridis, cols4all and paletteer names through one syntax,
with \code{reverse} and \code{range} trimming.
\item \strong{Bivariate maps.} \code{\link[=classify_bivariate]{classify_bivariate()}}, \code{\link[=scale_fill_bivariate]{scale_fill_bivariate()}}
and \code{\link[=legend_bivariate]{legend_bivariate()}} take two variables from raw numbers to a
coloured map with a grid legend.
\item \strong{Map-shaped defaults.} Out-of-bounds values squish rather than vanish,
\code{\link[=trim_limits]{trim_limits()}} tames outliers, \code{\link[=symmetric_limits]{symmetric_limits()}} keeps a diverging
scale honest, and \code{\link[=label_map]{label_map()}} writes legend labels the way a legend is
normally written. \code{\link[=theme_ggcarto]{theme_ggcarto()}} is a complete map theme.
\item \strong{Defensible choices.} The package shows its working, in the order
\emph{inspect, choose, classify, map, diagnose}: \code{\link[=compare_breaks]{compare_breaks()}} and
\code{\link[=plot_breaks]{plot_breaks()}} draw the breaks on the distribution; \code{\link[=class_guide]{class_guide()}}
suggests a number of classes with its reasons; \code{\link[=class_summary]{class_summary()}}
tabulates the classes; \code{\link[=check_palette]{check_palette()}} and \code{\link[=plot_palette]{plot_palette()}} test a
palette for map use; \code{\link[=diagnose_map]{diagnose_map()}} reads every decision back off the
finished plot and says what a reader would get wrong.
}
